  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Patchy/areas of frost possible across northern \nareas later tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will build across the region overnight with clear\nskies and light winds. This will result in favorable conditions\nfor radiational cooling. Low temperatures will range from the \nmid to upper 30s across much of northern Maine where a Frost\nAdvisory has been issued. Patchy/areas of frost are possible \nlate tonight in the normally colder low lying locations. Patchy \nriver valley fog is also possible late tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nModerate rain showers expected Sunday afternoon into Monday \nmorning but should have minimal impact on drought expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA northern stream trough tracks across southeastern Canada from\nSunday afternoon into late Sunday night, then crosses Maine\nfrom early Monday morning into early Monday evening. At the\nsurface a cold front will approach Sunday, then cross the area\nSunday night and Monday morning. This should bring some \nscattered to possibly numerous showers to the region from late \nSunday afternoon into Monday morning. The strongest forcing will\nbe across the North, with that area in the left rear quadrant \nof a developing 110+ kt upper jet. Instability below 200 J/kg is\nexpected across this area with mid-level lapse rates of 6.5-7 \nC/km. So some elevated convection, possibly even a rumble of \nthunder (best chance for the latter is across the North Woods, \nwith the best forcing) is possible.\n\nPrecipitable water values should range from 1.25 to 1.5 inches \nacross the North and 1.5 to 1.75 inches elsewhere. Given the \nvery dry antecedent conditions, other than possibly some ponding\nof water on roadways, no significant impacts are expected. As \nof now, this is not expected to have any appreciable impact on \nongoing drought across the region.",
